<figure class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-2 border-ink p-4 sm:p-6">
  <p class="font-bold">The horn gets narrower for ever. Its surface never ends.</p>
  <svg
    viewBox="0 0 300 146"
    class="mx-auto w-full max-w-sm"
    role="img"
    aria-labelledby="horn-title horn-desc"
  >
    <title id="horn-title">An endless tapering horn</title>
    <desc id="horn-desc">
      The wide left opening tapers toward the right, continuing without end. In the ideal
      mathematical model, its enclosed volume is finite and its surface area infinite.
    </desc>
    <path
      d="M24 14 C80 40 100 56 170 62 S255 67 288 68 L288 74 C255 75 205 79 170 81 C100 86 80 100 24 126 Z"
      fill="#387a91"
      fill-opacity=".25"
      stroke="currentColor"
      stroke-width="2.5"
    />
    <ellipse
      cx="24"
      cy="70"
      rx="9"
      ry="56"
      stroke="currentColor"
      stroke-width="2"
      fill="#d7a13d"
      fill-opacity=".3"
    />
    <path
      d="M40 70 H286"
      fill="none"
      stroke="currentColor"
      stroke-dasharray="7 7"
      opacity=".5"
    />
    <g fill="currentColor" font-size="16">
      <text x="150" y="38">
        Finite volume
      </text>
      <text x="150" y="112">
        Infinite surface
      </text>
      <text x="190" y="136">
        Continues →
      </text>
    </g>
  </svg>
  <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-base">
    <div class="border border-ink p-3">
      <strong>Filling</strong>
      <p>A finite amount of ideal liquid.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="border border-ink p-3">
      <strong>Same-thickness coat</strong>
      <p>Would need infinite paint.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <figcaption class="text-base leading-normal">
    This is a schematic of the mathematical shape. A layer allowed to get thinner without limit
    can touch the entire wall using finite volume. A coating of fixed positive thickness cannot.
    Real paint cannot reach arbitrarily far inside.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
